    \label{fig:mrr-rerank}
\end{figure}


\subsection{The Needle in the Haystack}
\label{sec:needle-in-the-haystack}
In reranking, MMR-RBO can provide limited assistance in identifying the best query in a batch, but the parameter $\lambda$ proves extremely sensitive to the number of questions being generated. We observe a positive monotonic relationship between MRR and MMR-RBO (Figure~\ref{fig:rerank-corr}) by evaluating the average Fisher-transformed Spearman correlation across question batches (Table~\ref{tab:correlation}). As the number of questions per batch increases, the relationship between these values becomes significant across more batches as well. This suggests that, while there is an observed positive correlation between variables, MMR-RBO is highly susceptible to variance in small datasets. Generating more questions makes it easier to identify which questions outperform the extractive baseline.

