\begin{abstract}
We describe a method for rendering multiple extremally charged black holes using an analogous system in classical optics, simplifying the prerequisite mathematics for generating accurate images. A primary goal of this work is to showcase a suite of techniques from geometry and relativity that may be of interest to illustrators and artists.
\end{abstract}

\vspace{5mm}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\noindent Accurately ray-tracing a scene that contains several black holes is, in principle, straightforward: follow every lightlike geodesic backward along the camera's past light cone until it strikes an object (Figure \ref{fig:lightcones}). In practice however, the task explodes into solving nonlinear ODEs on a complicated spacetime (itself the solution of nonlinear PDE), an enterprise that is both fragile and computationally demanding.

% Let us begin with the simplifying assumption that you, dear reader, are interested in rendering accurate images of complex spacetimes including multiple black holes.  Familiarity with standard raytracing suggests a natural relativistic generalization: trace geodesics backwards along an observer's past light cone, and compute intersections with the worldlines of objects in the scene.  But implementing this generally requires writing a four dimensional raytracer on a numerically-described spacetime, which presents various challenges.

