\vspace{-2mm}

In this note we exhibit a situation where these difficulties can be avoided, thanks to a beautiful \emph{exact} solution to general relativity containing charged black holes in static equilibrium. Exploiting its symmetries with tricks of Lorentzian geometry, we reduce the null-geodesic equation to a three-dimensional system corresponding to light rays in flat space with variable optical media. This allows the exact rendering of black holes in a \emph{Euclidean} rendering engine with advanced optics - no relativistic simulator required. As examples, all figures were produced with my custom renderer, by implementing variable indices of refraction.

