\section{Mathematics of magnetic field evolution \label{Flux constraint} }


The important constraints that arise from the evolution of the poloidal magnetic flux come directly from Faraday's law, $\partial\vec{B}/\partial t=-\vec{\nabla}\times \vec{E}$ and the general representation of a magnetic field in a tokamak or stellarator.

The magnetic field in a tokamak or stellarator can always be written using the toroidal magnetic flux $\psi$, the poloidal flux $\psi_p$, a toroidal angle $\varphi$ and a poloidal angle $\theta$ \cite{B-contra,Boozer:RMP},
\begin{eqnarray}
\vec{B} &=& \vec{\nabla}\psi \times \vec{\nabla}\frac{\theta}{2\pi} + \vec{\nabla}\frac{\varphi}{2\pi} \times \vec{\nabla}\psi_p(\psi,\theta,\varphi,t). \label{B-contra}
\end{eqnarray}
When used as coordinates $(\psi,\theta,\varphi)$ provide many simplifications.  The mathematics of general coordinates are explained in the appendix to Reference \cite{Boozer:RMP} and starts with the function $\vec{x}(\psi,\theta,\varphi,t)$, which gives the locations of $(\psi,\theta,\varphi)$  points in ordinary Cartesian coordinates.  To avoid subtleties, the coordinate Jacobian should have neither zeroes nor infinities.  The  Jacobian is
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{J} &\equiv&\frac{1}{ (\vec{\nabla}\psi \times \vec{\nabla}\theta)\cdot\vec{\nabla}\varphi}\\
&=& \frac{1}{\vec{B}\cdot\vec{\nabla}\varphi},
\end{eqnarray}
which is $2\pi$ times the major radius divided by the toroidal magnetic field strength.

The magnetic field lines are given by equations of the form $d\theta/d\varphi = \vec{B}\cdot\vec{\nabla}\theta/\vec{B}\cdot\vec{\nabla}\varphi$.  Equation (\ref{B-contra}) implies the field line equations have the Hamiltonian form \cite{B-contra,Boozer:RMP}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d\theta}{d\varphi} &=& + \frac{\partial \psi_p}{\partial\psi}; \\
\frac{d\psi}{d\varphi} &=& -\frac{\partial \psi_p}{\partial\theta}.
\end{eqnarray}

Mathematics alone implies that the three components of the electric field can be represented as
\begin{equation}\vec{E}+\vec{u}\times\vec{B} = -\vec{\nabla}\Phi + V_\ell \vec{\nabla}\frac{\varphi}{2\pi}. \label{E} \end{equation}  
The potential $\Phi$ can always be chosen so the loop voltage $V_\ell$ satisfies $\vec{B}\cdot\vec{\nabla}V_\ell=0$.  When $V_\ell=0$, $\vec{u}$ can be interpreted as the magnetic field line velocity \cite{Newcomb:1958}.

Faraday's law and Equation (\ref{B-contra}) for the magnetic field imply
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\vec{E} + \vec{u}\times\vec{B}= -\vec{\nabla}\Phi + \left(\frac{\partial\psi_p}{\partial t}\right)_{\psi,\theta,\varphi}\vec{\nabla}\frac{\varphi}{2\pi}   \label{psi_p dot}  \\
&& \vec{u}  \equiv   \frac{\partial \vec{x}(\psi,\theta,\varphi,t)}{\partial t}.
%&& V_\ell(\psi,\theta,\varphi,t) \equiv \frac{\partial\psi_p}{\partial t}.
\end{eqnarray}
The proof is a non-trivial application of the theory of general coordinates and is given in the appendix to  \cite{Boozer:RMP}.   A comparison of Equations (\ref{E}) and (\ref{psi_p dot}) gives insights on the interpretation of the terms.   The potential $\Phi$ can always be chosen so $\vec{B}\cdot\vec{\nabla}(\partial\psi_p/\partial t)=0$.  When $\Phi$ can be chosen so $\partial\psi_p/\partial t =0$, the magnetic evolution is said to be ideal, and the velocity of the coordinates $(\psi,\theta,\varphi)$ through space, $\vec{u}$, is the velocity of the magnetic field lines.


Where the magnetic field lines lie on magnetic surfaces, $\psi_p$ can be chosen to be a function of $\psi$ and time.  The toroidal magnetic flux enclosed by a magnetic surface is $\psi$ and the magnetic flux that passes through the central hole in the torus of a magnetic surface, Figure \ref{fig: B}, is the poloidal magnetic flux $\psi_p(\psi,t)$.  The two partial derivatives of $\psi_p(\psi,t)$ are of fundamental importance
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial \psi_p(\psi,t)}{\partial t} &=&V_\ell(\psi,t); \label{Loop voltage}\\
\frac{\partial \psi_p(\psi,t)}{\partial\psi} &=&\iota(\psi,t),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\iota \equiv d\theta/d\varphi = 1/q(\psi,t)$.

When magnetic surfaces are broken and the magnetic field lines become chaotic, the helicity $K$, not the poloidal flux, becomes the quantity of primary importance.  Appendix \ref{helicity} discusses the helicity.   The representation of the magnetic field of Equation (\ref{B-contra}) is valid whether the magnetic field is chaotic or not.  Complications due to the guage freedom of the vector potential are eliminated from $dK/dt$ when the integration volume is bounded by perfectly conducting surfaces.   During fast phenomena, such as disruptions, both the magnetic surfaces that remain intact and the chamber walls can be approximated as perfect conductors.

The magnetic helicity $K$ between two perfectly conducting magnetic surfaces $\psi_{in}$ and $\psi_{out}$ is defined by
\begin{eqnarray}
K &\equiv& \int \vec{A}\cdot\vec{B} d^3 x \label{K-def}\\
&=&\int \Big( \psi \frac{\partial\psi_p}{\partial\psi} -\psi_p\Big)\frac{d\psi d\theta d\varphi}{(2\pi)^2} \\
&=& \psi \psi_p\Big]_{\psi_{in}}^{\psi_{out}} - 2 \int_{\psi_{in}}^{\psi_{out}} d\psi \oint\frac{ d\theta d\varphi}{(2\pi)^2} \psi_p\\
\frac{dK}{dt} &=& - 2  \int_{\psi_{in}}^{\psi_{out}} d\psi \oint\frac{ d\theta d\varphi}{(2\pi)^2} \frac{\partial\psi_p}{\partial t}. \label{dK/dt}
\end{eqnarray}

Two situations of interest are discussed in  Appendix \ref{helicity}:  (1) When the chaotic region is bounded by both an inner and an outer perfectly conducting surface  (2) When only an outer perfectly conducting surface, which could be the chamber walls as during a major disruption, bounds the chaotic region.  The second or disruption case is of primary interest.  When the pre-disruption profile of the net parallel current is parabolic $j_{||} \propto (1 -\psi/\psi_b)$ with  a conducting wall is at the plasma boundary $\psi_b$, then the poloidal flux within the plasma drops to 2/3 of its pre-disruption value, but the plasma current increases by a factor of 4/3 from its strength before the disruption. 

When the magnetic axis is unbroken, the poloidal flux enclosed by the axis is unchanged by a rapid helicity-conserving interaction, nor is the poloidal flux changed through the hole of the torus defined a magnetic surface that remains intact.


