\draw [violet] (0.5,1)--(1.5,1);
\draw [violet] (0.5,0.9)--(0.5,1.1);
\draw [violet] (1.5,0.9)--(1.5,1.1);
\draw node at (1,0.7) {$\color{violet}A_{I}$};
\end{tikzpicture}$$


Our key observation is that operators localized near intervals of the physical boundary form subalgebras $B_{I}\subseteq A_{I}$, which assemble into a global subalgebra $B\subseteq A$ of the quasi-local algebra of observables which we call the \textit{physical boundary subalgebra}\footnote{this will later have a formal abstract definition}.

