\title{Potentials in recurrent networks: a survey}
%\title{Every recurrent network has a potential tending to infinity}
\author{Asaf Nachmias\footnote{Department of Mathematical Sciences, Tel Aviv University, Israel. }}
%\address{}
\author{Yuval Peres\footnote{Beijing Institute of Mathematical Sciences and Applications, Beijing, China. }}

\begin{abstract}   A nonnegative function on the vertices of an infinite graph $G$ which vanishes at a distinguished vertex $\o$, has Laplacian $1$ at $\o$, and is harmonic at all other vertices is called a potential. We survey basic properties of potentials in recurrent networks. In particular, we show that potentials are Lipschitz with respect to the effective resistance metric, and   
 if the potential is unique, then there is a determinantal formula for the harmonic measures from infinity.  We also infer from the von Neumann minimax theorem that there always exists a potential tending to infinity.  

%It is known that any recurrent network in which the weighted uniform spanning tree has one end admits harmonic measures from infinity. We show that the converse is false, by putting suitable conductances on $\mathbb{Z}^5$.
\end{abstract}
%In rooted networks with a unique potential there is a natural notion of random walk conditioned to tend to infinity, and we show that if two such walks   in $G$ intersect finitely often, then the uniform spanning tree in $G$ has multiple ends.  

