The analytical methodology presented in this work has general application to any information associated with detection events beyond pulse height, such as pulse quality, but we focus on the specific addition of exact time information of each detection event. For application of this analysis technique to continuous monitoring system, similar assumptions would need to be made about filter particulate signal and background, however, a non-well geometry (possibly lower energy resolution) detector and the presence of radioxenon background would need to be assumed. As shown later in our results, high background is the regime on which our method most outperforms the baseline, so the benefit in continuous monitoring could be sizeable. Additionally, radioxenon exhibits coincidence decays ($\beta$-$\gamma$ decays within $\mu s$), so if the detector were given beta detection capabilities (such as a thin window scintillation detector), even greater gains might be possible in a continuous monitoring scenario.

