\begin{abstract}

When applying LLM-based code generation to software development projects that follow a feature-driven or rapid application development approach, it becomes necessary to estimate the functional correctness of the generated code in the absence of test cases. Just as a user selects a relevant document from a ranked list of retrieved ones, a software generation workflow requires a developer to choose (and potentially refine) a generated solution from a ranked list of alternative solutions, ordered by their posterior likelihoods.
%
This implies that estimating the quality of a ranked
list -- akin to estimating ``relevance'' for query performance prediction (QPP) in IR -- is also crucial for generative software development, where quality is defined in terms of ``functional correctness''. In this paper, we propose an in-context learning (ICL) based approach
for code quality estimation. Our findings demonstrate that providing few-shot examples of functionally correct code from a training set enhances the performance of existing QPP approaches as well as a zero-shot-based approach for code quality estimation.














% In software development, ensuring the functional correctness of code is essential because poorly written or incorrect code can lead to bugs, security vulnerabilities, and system failures.
% Large language model (LLM)-based code generation when applied to software development projects operating in a feature-driven development mode or rapid application development mode, requires estimating the functional correctness of the generated code without the presence of test-cases.
% % With the increasing use of large language models (LLMs) for feature-driven development of software code generation, it is important to estimate the quality of these automatically generated solutions in terms of functional correctness without the presence of test-cases.
% Similar to a user finding a relevant document from a ranked list of retrieved ones, a software generation workflow requires a user (software developer) to select (and possibly post-edit) a generated solution from a list of alternative solutions ranked by their posterior likelihoods. This means that estimating the quality of a ranked list -- which is a standard task in IR, where quality refers to ``relevance'' -- is also an important task for generative software development, with the notion of quality being grounded in ``functional correctness''.
% In this paper, we compare the performance of existing QPP approaches with an in-context learning (ICL)-based code quality estimation approach, and show that few-shot examples of functionally correct codes from a training set can improve on the existing QPP approaches and also on a zero-shot based approach for code quality estimation performance. 

% With the advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) for code generation, it has become increasingly important to estimate the quality of these automatically generated solutions. Estimating the quality of LLM-generated code helps to prioritize the most correct and efficient solutions over incorrect ones which helps to streamline the testing process ensuring high code quality. We propose a novel approach to estimate the functional correctness of LLM generated solutions that uses training examples for few-shot learning which extends over existing zero-shot LLM based code evaluation method. We use relatively smaller LLM model for code evaluation which is practical to use in real world. Our method does not rely on reference code or test cases for quality estimation. It also generalizes across programming languages.

\end{abstract}

