In the following lemmas we push the lower bound up by a combination of structural arguments and of exhaustive computer search.
Since the total number of edges in $K_{18}$ is $153$, it is obvious that the number of triples in a $2$-$(18, \{3,4\},1)$ covering is always odd. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18alphage9}
		$D(18,\{3,4\})\ge 30$, or equivalently, $\alpha\ge 9$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	According to Lemma \ref{lem:lb-v6v9}, it suffices to show that $D(18, \{3,4\})\neq 29$, which is equivalent to $\alpha\neq 7$. 

	For contradiction, assume there is a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$ with 7 $K_3$'s (and thus 22 $K_4$'s). For any vertex $x$ of $K_{18}$, $\alpha_x\equiv 1\pmod{3}$ by Lemma \ref{lem:alphax-k18}. More concretely, due to the limited number of vertices, $\alpha_x\in \{1,4,7\}$ for any vertex $x$. If there is some vertex $u$ with $\alpha_u\ge 7$, then the total number of triples satisfies
	\begin{align}\label{eq:k18counttriples}
		\alpha = \frac{\sum_{x\in V(K_{18})}\alpha_x}{3}\ge \frac{1\cdot 7+17\cdot 1}{3}=8,
	\end{align}
	which contradicts with our assumption! Therefore, $\alpha_x\in \{1,4\}$ for each vertex $x$ of the $K_{18}$, and thus there must be one vertex $u_0$ with $\alpha_{u_0}=4$ and $\alpha_x=1$ for any other vertex $x$. In other words, $u_0$ is contained in 4 triples, and there are 3 more independent triples in the decomposition. After removing the 7 triangles from $K_{18}$, an ILP search shows that the remaining subgraph can not be decomposed into $K_4$'s. We include the ILP search code below. For the definition of the search function with Gurobi, see Listing \ref{src:gurobi-ilp}. 
	\begin{verbatim}
		order = 18
		block_sizes = [4]
		removed_edges = [
			[1, 2], [1, 3], [2, 3],
			[1, 4], [1, 5], [4, 5],
			[1, 6], [1, 7], [6, 7],
			[1, 8], [1, 9], [8, 9],
			[10, 11], [10, 12], [11, 12],
			[13, 14], [13, 15], [14, 15],	
			[16, 17], [16, 18], [17, 18]	
		]
		excess = None
		solutions = gurobi_linear(order, block_sizes, removed_edges, excess)
	\end{verbatim}
	Therefore, $\alpha\neq 7$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18alphage11}
	$D(18,\{3,4\})\ge 31$, or equivalently, $\alpha\ge 11$.	
\end{lemma}

