\begin{proof}
For contradiction suppose the case \eqref{eq:c1} is possible. Then there are $t_3=6$ triples among $w_1,\cdots,w_7$. Denote by $H$ the union of these 6 triples and consider its complement $\overline{H}$ in the $K_7$-subgraph induced on vertices $w_1,\cdots,w_7$. Clearly, $\overline{H}$ is an even graph with $e(K_7)-6\cdot 3=3$ edges. Ignoring the isolated vertices in $\overline{H}$, we can easily deduce $\overline{H}\cong C_3$. The three edges in $\overline{H}$ provide the $t_2=3$ triples, and the four isolated vertices in $\overline{H}$ provide the $t_1=4$ triples, hence the distribution of triples in such a decomposition is unique, see Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-1}. This configuration is excluded by an ILP search on the remaining edges. 
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18alpha13c2}
	In a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$, if $\alpha=13$, then the case \eqref{eq:c2} is impossible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For contradiction suppose the case \eqref{eq:c2} is possible. Then there are $t_3=5$ triples among $w_1,\cdots,w_7$, so any feasible complement of the union of such 5 $K_3$'s in the $K_7$ must be an even graph of size 6. Using \texttt{nauty}, we generate all of the 3 mutually non-isomorphic even graphs $H$ of order 7 and size 6 (See Listing \ref{src:nauty-7-6}), as shown in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, where the vertex $i$ represents $w_{i+1}$ for $i\in\{0,1,\cdots,6\}$. We then check whether the complement $\overline{H}$ is decomposable by $K_3$ using an ILP search, and it turns out that the 3rd graph in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, i.e. $C_3+C_3$ has a complement which is not decomposable by $K_3$. 

	If $H$ is a bowtie as the 1st graph in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, then the vertices $w_3$ and $w_4$ are each contained in 3 triples in $\overline{H}$, so they each require an additional triple with two vertices among $y_1,\cdots,y_{11}$. This contradicts with $t_1=1$ in \eqref{eq:c2}. 

	If $H\cong C_6$ is a cycle as the 2nd graph in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, we take the 5 triples $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_4,w_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,w_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_5\}$, $\{w_5,w_6,w_7\}$ from $\overline{H}$, and we also notice that $w_4$ requires an additional triple, which we can take, without loss of generality, to be $\{w_4,y_1,y_2\}$. For each edge in $H$, we form a triple with a vertex among $y_1,\cdots,y_{11}$: $\{w_1,w_5,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_8\}$. Finally, we append a triple $\{y_9,y_{10},y_{11}\}$. This configuration of 13 triples (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-5}) is shown to be infeasible by an ILP search. 

	To sum up, the case \eqref{eq:c2} is impossible.
\end{proof}

