\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18a13c3}
	In a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$, if $\alpha=13$, then the case \eqref{eq:c3} is impossible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For contradiction suppose the case {\eqref{eq:c3}} is possible. Since $t_3=5$ as in the case \eqref{eq:c2}, we still consider the first two graphs $H$ in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, as in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:k18alpha13c2}. As a reminder, the complement $\overline{H}$ in the $K_7$ with vertices $w_1,\cdots,w_7$, where $w_{i}$ is represented by $i-1$ for $i\in\{1,2,\cdots,7\}$ in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, gives the union of the 5 triangles among $w_1,\cdots,w_7$. 

	If $H$ is a bowtie as the 1st graph in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, we take the 5 triples $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_4,w_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,w_5\}$, $\{w_3,w_5,w_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_7\}$ from $\overline{H}$, and $w_3$ and $w_4$ each need an additional triple, which we take, without loss of generality, to be $\{w_3,y_1,y_2\}$ and $\{w_4, y_3,y_4\}$. Since $t_2=5$ in \eqref{eq:c3}, we need to select select 5 edges out of 6 in $H$ to form triples with vertices among $y_1,\cdots,y_{11}$. By counting the triples containing each vertex, we deduce that precisely 1 edge incident to $w_7$ must not be chosen. Therefore, without loss of generality, we select all edges except $\{w_6,w_7\}$, and they form the triples $\{w_1,w_5,y_5\}$, $\{w_1,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_8\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_9\}$. We append a final triple for $w_6$: $\{w_6,y_{10},y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-6} up) is then excluded by an ILP search. 

	If $H\cong C_6$ is a cycle as the 2nd graph in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-6-e}, we take the 5 triples $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_4,w_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,w_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_5\}$, $\{w_5,w_6,w_7\}$ from $\overline{H}$, and we also notice that $w_4$ requires an additional triple, which we can take, without loss of generality, to be $\{w_4,y_1,y_2\}$. Since $t_2=5$ in \eqref{eq:c3}, we need to select 5 edges out of 6 in $H$ to form triples with vertices among $y_1,\cdots,y_{11}$. As $H\cong C_6$ is a cycle, we can take, without loss of generality, all edges of $H$ except $\{w_3,w_7\}$. Therefore, the triples can be taken to be $\{w_1,w_5,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_6\}$ and $\{w_3,w_6,y_7\}$. We append two triples for $w_3$ and $w_7$ respectively: $\{w_3,y_8,y_9\}$, $\{w_7,y_{10},y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-6} down) is then excluded by an ILP search. 

	To sum up, the case \eqref{eq:c3} is impossible.
\end{proof}

