\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18alpha13c4}
In a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$, if $\alpha=13$, then the case \eqref{eq:c4} is impossible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For contradiction suppose the case {\eqref{eq:c4}} is possible. Then there are $t_3=4$ triples among $w_1,\cdots,w_7$, so the complement of the union of these 4 $K_3$'s in the $K_7$ is an even graph of size 9. Using \texttt{nauty}, we generate all of the 6 mutually non-isomorphic even graphs $G_1, G_2, \cdots, G_6$ of order 7 and size 9 (See Listing \ref{src:nauty-7-9}), as shown in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-9-e}, where the vertex $i$ represents $w_{i+1}$ for $i\in\{0,1,\cdots,6\}$. We then check whether the complement $\overline{H}$ of $H \in \{ G_1, G_2, \cdots, G_6 \}$ is decomposable by $K_3$ by an ILP search, and it turns out that $G_1$, $G_2$ and $G_6$ in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-9-e} do not have a complement that is decomposable by $K_3$. So we deduce that up to isomorphism, 
	\begin{align*}
		H\in \{G_3,G_4,G_5\}.
	\end{align*}
	If $H\cong G_3$, then $w_4$ is already contained in 3 triples in $\overline{H}$, so it needs another triple formed with two vertices among $y_1,\cdots,y_{11}$, which is impossible for $t_1=0$ in \eqref{eq:c4}. 

	If $H\cong G_4$, then $w_7$ is incident to 6 edges in~$H$, but it is only contained in 4 triples. Therefore, at least 2 of the 6 edges in~$H$ do not contribute to triples. In other words, $|E(H)| = 9$ and $t_2\le 9-2=7$. However, $t_2=8$ in \eqref{eq:c4}, a contradiction!

	If $H\cong G_5$, we first take the 4 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_7\}$, $\{w_1,w_3,w_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_3,w_4\}$, $\{w_4,w_5,w_6\}$. Since $w_6$ and $w_7$ are each in 1 triple in $\overline{H}$, we need to take in $H$ 3 edges adjacent to $w_6$ and 3 adjacent to $w_7$ to form triples. Since $t_2=8$ in $\eqref{eq:c4}$, it is obvious that all edges in $H$ except $\{w_6,w_7\}$ contribute to triples, which we may take as: $\{w_1,w_4,y_1\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_2\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_3\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_5\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_7\}$ and $\{w_5,w_7,y_8\}$. We append a final triple out of the $K_7$: $\{y_9,y_{10},y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-7}) is then shown infeasible by an ILP search. 
\end{proof}

