\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18a13c5-1}
	In a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$, if $\alpha=13$ with the case \eqref{eq:c5} holds, then up to isomorphism $H\notin \{G_3,G_4\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For contradiction suppose the case \eqref{eq:c5} is possible. Since $t_3=4$ as in the case \eqref{eq:c4}, we still consider the graphs $H\in\{G_3,G_4,G_5\}$ in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-9-e}, as in the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:k18alpha13c4}. As a reminder, the complement $\overline{H}$ in the $K_7$ with vertices $w_1,\cdots,w_7$, where $w_{i}$ is represented by $i-1$ for $i\in\{1,2,\cdots,7\}$ in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-9-e}, gives the union of the 4 triangles among $w_1,\cdots,w_7$. 

	If $H\cong G_3$, we first take the 4 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_4,w_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,w_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_5\}$, and we form another triple for $w_4$: $\{w_4,y_1,y_2\}$. Notice that, $w_5,w_6,w_7$ each have 4 incident edges in $H$ and are each contained in 1 triple in $\overline{H}$. Since $t_2=7$ in \eqref{eq:c5}, we deduce that precisely 2 edges among $\{w_5,w_6\}$, $\{w_5,w_7\}$, and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ must not form a triple. Without loss of generality, we assume that all edges of $H$ except $\{w_5,w_6\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ contribute to triples, which may be taken as: $\{w_1,w_5, y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_8\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_9\}$. We finally append a triple for $w_6$: $\{w_6,y_{10}, y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-8}) is then shown infeasible by an ILP search. 

	If $H\cong G_4$, we first take the 4 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_5,w_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,w_6\}$ and, $\{w_3,w_4,w_5\}$. Since $\deg_{H}(w_7)=6$, we deduce that precisely 2 out of the 6 edges incident to $w_7$ must not contribute to triples. Up to isomorphism, there are two cases. 
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item These 2 edges are chosen in the same triangle in $H$. Without loss of generality, we assume that all edges except $\{w_3,w_7\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ contribute to triples, which we take as: $\{w_1,w_4,y_1\}$, $\{w_1,w_7,y_2\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_3\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_4\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_5\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_7\}$. We finally append two triples for $w_3$ and $w_6$ respectively: $\{w_3,y_8,y_9\}$, $\{w_6,y_{10}, y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-9} left) is then shown infeasible by an ILP search. 
		\item These 2 edges are chosen from different triangles in $H$. Without loss of generality, we assume that all edges except $\{w_1,w_7\}$ and $\{w_2,w_7\}$ contribute to triples, which we take as: $\{w_1,w_4,y_1\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_2\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_3\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_4\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_5\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_6,w_7,y_7\}$. We finally append two triples for $w_1$ and $w_2$ respectively: $\{w_1,y_8,y_9\}$, $\{w_2,y_{10}, y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-9} right) is then shown infeasible by an ILP search. 
	\end{enumerate} 
This concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

