\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18a13c5-2}
In a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$, if $\alpha=13$, then the case \eqref{eq:c5} is impossible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	Following Lemma \ref{lem:k18a13c5-1}, we continue to exclude the possibility that $H\cong G_5$. Assume that $H\cong G_5$, we first take the 4 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_7\}$, $\{w_1,w_3,w_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_3,w_4\}$, and $\{w_4,w_5,w_6\}$. Since $t_1=2$ in \eqref{eq:c5}, we discuss about which vertices in $H$ are contained in these 2 triples. 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item If at least one of the degree-4 vertices $w_6,w_7$ in $H$ is contained in such a triple, then, since they cannot be both, without loss of generality, we assume there is a triple $\{w_6, y_1,y_2\}$. Then the other vertex contained in the $t_1=2$ triples can be one of several possibilities. 
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item It is $w_3$, the unique common neighbour of $w_6$ and $w_7$. This triple can be taken to be $\{w_3,y_3,y_4\}$. We then deduce that all edges of $H$ except $\{w_3,w_6\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ contribute to the $t_2=7$ triples, which we may take to be $\{w_1,w_4,y_5\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_9\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-10} upper left) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
			\item It is adjacent to $w_6$ but not adjacent to $w_7$, and without loss of generality we assume it is $w_1$, and take a triple $\{w_1,y_3,y_4\}$. We then deduce that all edges of $H$ except $\{w_1,w_6\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ contribute to the $t_2=7$ triples, which we may take to be $\{w_1,w_4,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_9\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-10} upper right) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
		\end{enumerate}
		\item Else if $w_3$ is one of them, we may take the triple $\{w_3,y_1,y_2\}$. Then the other vertex contained in the $t_1=2$ triples has 2 possibilities. 
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item It coincides with $w_3$, i.e. $w_3$ is contained in another triple $\{w_3, y_3, y_4\}$, and we can deduce that all edges of $H$ except $\{w_3,w_6\}$ and $\{w_3,w_7\}$ contribute to the $t_2=7$ triples, which we may take to be $\{w_1,w_4,y_5\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_8\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_9\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_6,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-10} lower left) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
			\item It is one of the neighbours $w_6$, $w_7$ of $w_3$. This case, up to isomorphism, has been already considered. 
		\end{enumerate}
		\item Otherwise, they must be two adjacent vertices among $\{w_1,w_2,w_4,w_5\}$. Without loss of generality, we take two triples $\{w_1,y_1,y_2\}$ and $\{w_4,y_3,y_4\}$. Then we deduce that all edges of $H$ except $\{w_1,w_4\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ contribute to the $t_2=7$ triples, which we may take to be $\{w_1,w_6,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_9\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18alpha13-10} lower right) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
	\end{itemize}
        This concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

