\begin{lemma}\label{lem:k18a13c6}
	In a $\{K_3,K_4\}$-decomposition of $K_{18}$, if $\alpha=13$, then the case \eqref{eq:c6} is impossible.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For contradiction suppose the case \eqref{eq:c6} is possible. Then there are $t_3=3$ triples among $w_1,\cdots,w_7$, so the complement of the union of these 3 triangles in the $K_7$ is an even graph of size 12. Using \texttt{nauty}, we generate all of the 6 mutually non-isomorphic even graphs $H$ of order 7 and size 12 (See Listing \ref{src:nauty-7-12}), as shown in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-12-e}, where the vertex $i$ represents $w_{i+1}$ for $i\in\{0,1,\cdots,6\}$. We then check whether the complement $\overline{H}$ is decomposable by $K_3$ by an ILP search, and it turns out that the graphs $H_4,H_5,H_6$ in Figure \ref{fig:nauty-7-12-e} are not feasible. 

	If $H\cong H_1$, we first take the 3 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,w_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_5\}$. Since $t_2=9$ in \eqref{eq:c6}, we then need to remove 3 edges from $H_1$ that do not contribute to triples. Since $\deg_{H}(w_7)=6$, we need to remove at least 2 edges incident to $w_7$. Similarly, since $\deg_H(w_1)=\deg_H(w_5)=\deg_H(w_6)=4$, for each of them we need to remove at least 1 incident edge. Since $t_1=1$ in \eqref{eq:c6}, there are 2 cases.
	\begin{itemize}
		\item If the 3 edges $\{w_1,w_7\}$, $\{w_5,w_7\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$ incident to $w_7$ are removed, then the remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_4,y_1\}$, $\{w_1,w_5,y_2\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_3\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_5\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_6\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_7\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_8\}$, $\{w_5,w_6,y_9\}$. We finally append one triple for $w_7$: $\{w_7,y_{10}, y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} upper left) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
		\item Otherwise, only 2 edges incident to $w_7$ are removed, and without loss of generality, we assume $\{w_1,w_7\}$ remains, which forces us to remove another edge incident to $w_1$. There are 2 possibilities.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item If $\{w_1,w_4\}$ is removed, then we have to form a triple for $w_4$: $\{w_4,y_1,y_2\}$. The remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_5,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_1,w_7,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_9\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_5,w_6,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} upper middle) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
			\item Otherwise, without loss of generality, we may assume $\{w_1,w_5\}$ is removed, then we have to form a triple for $w_5$: $\{w_5,y_1,y_2\}$. The remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_4,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_1,w_7,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_7,y_9\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_5,w_6,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} upper right) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{itemize}

	If $H\cong H_2$, we first take the 3 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_5\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,w_7\}$. Since $t_2=9$ in \eqref{eq:c6}, we then need to remove 3 edges from $H_1$ that do not contribute to triples. Now that $H_2$ is actually a 4-regular graph of order 6, ignoring the isolated vertex $w_3$, we deduce that for each vertex other than $w_3$, precisely one of its incident edges must be removed. In other words, the 3 removed edges form a perfect matching of the 4-regular graph. Since a $K_6$ with 2 different perfect matchings removed is always isomorphic to $K_6-C_6$, we may take an arbitrary perfect matching: $\{w_1,w_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_6\}$, and $\{w_5,w_7\}$. Then the remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_5,y_1\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_2\}$, $\{w_1,w_7,y_3\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,y_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_6\}$, $\{w_4,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_4,w_7,y_8\}$, $\{w_5,w_6,y_9\}$. We finally append a triple for $w_3$: $\{w_3,y_{10}, y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} centre) is then excluded by an ILP search. 

	If $H\cong H_3$, we first take the 3 triples in $\overline{H}$: $\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$, $\{w_3,w_4,w_7\}$, $\{w_4,w_5,w_6\}$. Since $t_1=1$ in \eqref{eq:c6}, we need to choose one vertex that is contained in this triple. Inspecting the structure of $H_3$, there are 3 cases for this vertex. 
	\begin{itemize}
		\item If this vertex is $w_7$, we take the triple $\{w_7,y_1,y_2\}$, and 2 edges incident to $w_7$ are to be removed, of which one leads to either $w_1$ or $w_2$ and the other to either $w_5$ or $w_6$. This is because, for each vertex among $w_1,w_2,w_5,w_6$, precisely 1 incident edge is to remove. Without loss of generality, we remove $\{w_1,w_7\}$ and $\{w_6,w_7\}$, and the 3rd edges to remove is clearly $\{w_2,w_5\}$. Then the remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_4,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_5,y_4\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_5,y_9\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} lower left) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
		\item Else if this vertex is one of $w_1,w_2,w_5,w_6$, without loss of generality we assume it to be $w_1$ and take the triple $\{w_1,y_1,y_2\}$. Then 2 edges incident to $w_1$ are to be removed, of which one is $\{w_1,w_7\}$ and the other is either $\{w_1,w_5\}$ or $\{w_1,w_6\}$, since each of $w_2,w_5,w_6,w_7$ must have precisely 1 incident edge removed. Without loss of generality, we remove $\{w_1,w_5\}$, $\{w_1,w_7\}$ and consequently also $\{w_2,w_6\}$. Then the remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_4,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_4\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_5,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_7\}$, $\{w_3,w_5,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_6,y_9\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_6,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} lower middle) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
		\item Otherwise, this vertex is $w_3$ or $w_4$. Without loss of generality, we assume it to be $w_3$, and take the triple $\{w_3, y_1, y_2\}$. Then 1 edge incident to $w_3$ is to be removed, which we assume to be $\{w_3,w_6\}$. Then the other 2 edges to be removed can be assumed, without loss of generality, to be $\{w_1,w_7\}$ and $\{w_2,w_5\}$. Then the remaining 9 edges generate the triples: $\{w_1,w_4,y_3\}$, $\{w_1,w_5,y_4\}$, $\{w_1,w_6,y_5\}$, $\{w_2,w_4,y_6\}$, $\{w_2,w_6,y_7\}$, $\{w_2,w_7,y_8\}$, $\{w_3,w_5,y_9\}$, $\{w_5,w_7,y_{10}\}$, $\{w_6,w_7,y_{11}\}$. This configuration (See Figure \ref{fig:k18a13c6} lower right) is then excluded by an ILP search. 
	\end{itemize}
        This concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

